  "worked_solution": "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${A}^{T}=A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} & \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} & \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{100}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{100}[\\begin{aligned}90 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} & \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true since both statements I and II are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
